// Learner-friendly orientation excerpts from existing mastery teaching material.
window.DALP_LESSON_GUIDES = {"excel":{"1":{"title":"Excel Foundations & Navigation","goal":"Understand the Excel workspace and become comfortable moving around a workbook.","reason":"An operations analyst receives daily order exports. A clear workbook structure reduces formula mistakes and lets a colleague audit the numbers.","model":"Picture it this way: A well-designed workbook is a small information system: source rows enter in one place, calculations happen separately, and a dashboard displays the conclusion.","steps":["Open the CSV without changing the original file","Confirm each column contains one type of information and dates are true dates","Separate RawData, Calculations and Dashboard tabs"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create a 12-column data dictionary: field name, what it means and expected data type.","insight":"Read the expected result: Order 1001 is one row for a laptop sale: two units at 150000 each. Do not insert a subtotal row between transactions because PivotTables will count it as another record.","trap":"Merged headers, blank separator rows, and numbers stored as text break filters and PivotTables."},"2":{"title":"Data Entry, Formatting & Excel Tables","goal":"Turn raw entries into clean, professional, analysis-ready data.","reason":"Order logs grow daily. A manual formula range stops at yesterday’s last row, whereas a Table can extend calculated columns and references.","model":"Picture it this way: An Excel Table behaves like an expanding dataset, not a fixed rectangle of cells. Structured references name columns and stay readable when new orders arrive.","steps":["Select any cell in the data and press Ctrl+T with headers","Rename the table Sales","Add a Revenue column using =[@Quantity]*[@Unit_Price] with the actual header names"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add a new practice order and verify that the Revenue formula auto-fills; compare with a fixed A2:A6 range.","insight":"Read the expected result: Excel calculates 300000 for order 1001 and 40000 for order 1002. Status Data Validation helps manual entry but does not guarantee that imported rows are clean.","trap":"Data Validation improves entry quality, but imported data can still violate rules; audit it too."},"3":{"title":"Formulas, References & Essential Functions","goal":"Use Excel to calculate results instead of calculating manually.","reason":"Finance often asks for tax, targets and profit; formulas must handle fill-down correctly and preserve fixed assumptions.","model":"Picture it this way: A relative reference is a moving instruction; an absolute reference is an anchor. In sales work you often multiply different quantities by one shared VAT rate.","steps":["Enter a rate in one labelled cell","Use $ to lock that reference when copying a formula","Validate copied formulas with one hand-calculated order"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Write the formulas for quantity×price, quantity×unit cost and their difference; test with order 1001.","insight":"Read the expected result: If VAT is 7.5% and an order has revenue 300000, tax would be 22500; the net sale treatment depends on whether prices already include VAT.","trap":"Using A2 instead of $A$2 for a single global rate changes the rate as the formula is copied."},"4":{"title":"Logical, Conditional & Lookup Functions","goal":"Answer business questions with criteria and bring information from other tables.","reason":"Order reviews require statuses, thresholds and product categories from a lookup table.","model":"Picture it this way: IF is a fork in the road. SUMIFS groups only values meeting explicit rules. XLOOKUP or VLOOKUP attaches information from a reliable reference list.","steps":["Use IF to set returned orders' recognized revenue to zero","Use SUMIFS to add completed East revenue","Inspect lookup keys before pulling segment or region from a separate table"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Make a missing-lookup flag with IFERROR or ISNA; do not silently display zero for unknown customers.","insight":"Read the expected result: East completed revenue equals 300000 + 16000 = 316000; the East returned order worth 24000 is excluded. Excel 2016 users may need INDEX/MATCH or VLOOKUP instead of XLOOKUP.","trap":"VLOOKUP without FALSE can return approximate matches and silently misclassify products."},"5":{"title":"Data Cleaning & Preparation","goal":"Fix messy data before analysis — one of the most important Excel skills.","reason":"In real feeds, spaces, duplicates, invalid dates and inconsistent capitalization can fragment business categories.","model":"Picture it this way: Cleaning is not deleting anything that looks inconvenient. You preserve raw evidence, fix documented issues in a copy and test whether the change affected KPIs.","steps":["List the field-level quality rules","Trim extra spaces, validate numeric and date types, and inspect duplicates","Compare record counts and completed revenue before/after the cleaned view"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create a small issue log showing field, original value, correction, reason and validation result.","insight":"Read the expected result: Returned order 1003 must remain in the dataset for audit; it is simply excluded by the completed-sales business rule.","trap":"PROPER should not automatically change company acronyms or official names; get a business rule first."},"6":{"title":"Sorting, Filtering & Quick Analysis","goal":"Explore data, identify patterns, and highlight important results.","reason":"Sales managers filter geography and status during daily reviews; accidental partial-range sorting can detach quantities from the wrong orders.","model":"Picture it this way: Filtering is a viewing decision; deleting is a data change. Excel's SUBTOTAL reacts to filtered rows while plain SUM may include all rows.","steps":["Convert the range to a Table","Filter Status to Completed and Region to West","Apply SUBTOTAL to visible revenue and cross-check order IDs"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Sort by revenue descending, then restore original order by OrderID and explain why the totals remain unchanged.","insight":"Read the expected result: The visible West completed orders are 1002 and 1005, worth 40000 and 150000, for a total of 190000.","trap":"Sorting a single column independently of the rest of a record destroys row integrity."},"7":{"title":"PivotTables, PivotCharts & Slicers","goal":"Summarize hundreds or thousands of rows without writing complex formulas.","reason":"A 1,000-row sales log is difficult to interpret row-by-row. A PivotTable groups regions, months and products without manual SUMIFS for every combination.","model":"Picture it this way: A PivotTable answers 'how much by what category?' without editing each source row. The choice between % of Grand Total and % of Row Total changes the meaning.","steps":["Insert a PivotTable from Sales","Place Region in Rows and completed Revenue in Values","Use Show Values As → % of Grand Total and label it clearly"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Build a second PivotTable showing channel shares and explain why adding percentages directly can mislead.","insight":"Read the expected result: East's share is 316000 ÷ 578000 ≈ 54.67%. The percentages should add to 100% across the three regions when the same filter applies.","trap":"Sum of a percentage column is usually not a weighted rate; PivotTables require a correct denominator."},"8":{"title":"Charts & Data Visualization","goal":"Choose the right visual for the question and make charts easy to understand.","reason":"Stakeholders should identify what changed and where to act within seconds.","model":"Picture it this way: A chart is a comparison tool: bars compare categories, lines show time order, and a KPI card communicates one number.","steps":["State what decision the chart supports","Choose the visual based on category, trend or target","Check the title, units, zero baseline and accessibility contrast"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Redesign an unlabeled revenue chart with a descriptive title, currency units and a note that only Completed orders are counted.","insight":"Read the expected result: The region bar chart should visibly place East above West above North. A pie chart may hide the size difference when categories are numerous.","trap":"Truncated bar axes visually exaggerate differences; charts without denominator labels mislead."},"9":{"title":"Dashboard Planning, Design & Interactivity","goal":"Combine KPIs, charts, and filters into one clean reporting page.","reason":"A dashboard needs a defined audience, a small set of critical KPIs and clear interactions rather than a wall of charts.","model":"Picture it this way: A dashboard is a decision path, not a collage. Put the headline outcome first, drivers beneath it, then details for verification.","steps":["Define three top KPIs and their exact filters","Arrange trend and category drivers below KPI cards","Connect slicers and test that each interactive view reconciles"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Write a two-sentence manager's summary of February's revenue decrease without claiming a cause.","insight":"Read the expected result: The default training view shows 578000 completed revenue, 135000 profit and 23.36% gross margin. Every slicer state should retain a meaningful title.","trap":"Dashboard KPIs may disagree if one uses all orders and another excludes returned orders."},"10":{"title":"Final Excel Dashboard Project","goal":"Build and present an end-to-end interactive sales dashboard.","reason":"A finished analytical workbook should survive a new batch of rows and allow another analyst to reproduce every figure.","model":"Picture it this way: Portfolio work demonstrates repeatability: another analyst should be able to replace the CSV and understand the formulas without a guided tour.","steps":["Provide a raw-data sheet, transformation notes and calculation sheet","Build a clean one-page dashboard with filters","Audit totals, broken formulas, blank keys and refresh behavior"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Hand over an Excel workbook and a short QA checklist explaining what you verified and how.","insight":"Read the expected result: Acceptance checks: five Completed orders; 578000 recognized revenue; 135000 profit. Your evidence should include at least one failed-data scenario.","trap":"Do not submit an attractive dashboard with broken structured references or unrefreshed Pivots."}},"sql":{"1":{"title":"SQL Foundations & First Queries","goal":"Understand relational data and write your first SELECT statements.","reason":"A retail manager asks which orders were completed. A database stores repeated transactions consistently, and SQL retrieves the precise rows required for the decision.","model":"Picture it this way: Think of a database table as a register: each row is one order, and each column answers one fixed question about that order. The order ID identifies a record; it is not a calculation.","steps":["Open the orders table and inspect column names","Ask what one row represents before doing any sums","Choose only the columns needed, restrict the completed orders, then sort by order_id"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Change LIMIT 3 to LIMIT 5 and explain why five rows qualify even though the input has six.","insight":"Read the expected result: In the first displayed record, quantity 2 and unit price 150000 describe order 1001; the query has not yet multiplied them. LIMIT 3 limits the display, not the actual database.","trap":"Without ORDER BY, LIMIT does not guarantee which three rows are returned."},"2":{"title":"Filtering, Conditions & Sorting","goal":"Return only the rows that answer the business question.","reason":"Teams often ask for one region, one status, and a minimum transaction size; incorrect AND/OR logic quietly changes reported sales.","model":"Picture it this way: A WHERE filter is a set of admission rules for individual rows. The database checks those rules before performing any totals; ORDER BY decides how the surviving rows are displayed.","steps":["State each condition in plain language","Add parentheses around combined AND/OR tests","Sort the filtered result by a meaningful column and then a stable tie-break"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Show completed orders from East or West; then change the logic to East AND West and describe why no single-region order qualifies.","insight":"Read the expected result: Compare the order IDs returned, not just the count. The same five completed transactions can appear in different display orders without changing the business answer.","trap":"WHERE region='East' OR region='West' AND status='Completed' also admits returned East orders."},"3":{"title":"Aggregations & Grouped Analysis","goal":"Turn transaction rows into business summaries.","reason":"A performance dashboard must reconcile to finance: one returned order must not inflate completed-order revenue.","model":"Picture it this way: An aggregate compresses many qualifying orders into a summary. Decide the group (region) and the unit (currency value) before writing SUM, AVG or COUNT.","steps":["Filter to completed sales so returns are not treated as positive revenue","Calculate revenue per order as quantity × unit_price","Group by region and reconcile region totals back to 578000"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add profit per region, reconcile to 135000, and explain the difference between gross revenue and profit.","insight":"Read the expected result: East 316000 + West 190000 + North 72000 = 578000. These are gross order values; profit uses a different formula: quantity × (unit_price − unit_cost).","trap":"COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) ignores NULLs. Also never SUM a rate such as margin percentage."},"4":{"title":"Joining Tables","goal":"Combine related data from normalized tables.","reason":"Order records may hold a customer_id while the customer segment sits in a separate table; analyses need reliable dimension joins.","model":"Picture it this way: A JOIN is like looking up a customer’s segment using a customer ID. The danger is that matching two or more customer rows to one order can copy its revenue multiple times.","steps":["Write down the unique key on each side of the join","Check customer IDs for duplicates before joining","Compare order count and revenue before and after the join"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create an unmatched-customer report using LEFT JOIN and WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL; state what zero means.","insight":"Read the expected result: A LEFT JOIN preserves orders even when no customer record matches. The segment is extra context; the order amount must not change because of the lookup.","trap":"Do not join on non-unique names or forget the ON clause; check row counts and missing keys."},"5":{"title":"CASE Expressions, NULLs & Data Cleaning","goal":"Create business classifications and handle missing values safely.","reason":"Reports routinely distinguish completed, returned and pending transactions, plus unknown values that must not silently fall into a valid bucket.","model":"Picture it this way: NULL means 'not recorded' rather than zero or a blank string. A CASE expression is a decision tree: the first true condition wins, and ELSE handles everything else.","steps":["Count the raw status values","Specify business labels for Completed and Returned","Keep an ELSE category so new or missing statuses are visible"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add an AuditFlag label for NULL status using IS NULL, then describe why '= NULL' does not work.","insight":"Read the expected result: The example classifies orders 1001 and 1002 as Recognised, and order 1003 as Reversal. This describes the rule; it does not modify stored statuses.","trap":"WHERE status <> 'Completed' does not capture NULL statuses; test IS NULL explicitly."},"6":{"title":"Subqueries & Common Table Expressions","goal":"Break complex analysis into understandable steps.","reason":"Auditors and colleagues should be able to trace a KPI back to its qualifying transactions instead of interpreting a single oversized query.","model":"Picture it this way: A CTE is a named, temporary query result used within one SQL statement. Think of it as a worksheet tab that you can refer to later in the same calculation.","steps":["Create completed_sales as transaction-level rows","Check that the CTE contains five orders","Aggregate those rows by region in the outer query"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add a second CTE for regional totals, then retrieve only regions whose completed revenue exceeds 100000.","insight":"Read the expected result: The final regional numbers must equal the earlier grouped query. A CTE improves readability, but it does not automatically make the engine faster.","trap":"A CTE is not automatically cached or faster; benchmark the actual execution plan."},"7":{"title":"Window Functions for Analytical SQL","goal":"Calculate rankings, running totals, and prior-period comparisons without collapsing rows.","reason":"Top-customer reporting and monthly running totals need calculations that do not collapse the original records.","model":"Picture it this way: GROUP BY reduces rows to one summary per group; a window function adds context without removing the original rows. Picture a ranking column written beside each order.","steps":["Partition by customer so each customer has a separate sequence","Order by date descending and add order_id to break ties","Assign ROW_NUMBER and keep rank 1 only when you need the latest order"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Use DENSE_RANK on a regional revenue summary and explain what happens if two regions tie.","insight":"Read the expected result: Ngozi has two completed orders. Her February 18 order ranks ahead of her February 9 order, yet both appear in the unfiltered window result.","trap":"Window ORDER BY and final output ORDER BY serve different purposes. Missing tie-breaks make latest-row logic unstable."},"8":{"title":"Date Analysis & Dashboard-Ready Queries","goal":"Prepare clean analytical result sets for BI tools and recurring reporting.","reason":"Leadership needs January and February comparisons; grouping only by month name mixes years and creates misleading trends.","model":"Picture it this way: Time analysis starts by placing each order in an accurate calendar bucket. A month is not just 'January': the year matters, and partial months cannot always be compared fairly.","steps":["Extract YYYY-MM from the transaction date","Exclude returned orders consistently","Group revenue by month and compare complete periods"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add a previous_month column with LAG and explain why the first row has no previous comparison.","insight":"Read the expected result: January contributes 340000 and February 238000. The February change is −102000, or −30% of January; the tiny fictional sample cannot justify a trend forecast.","trap":"Beware string-sorted month names and using current-month partial data against a complete prior month."},"9":{"title":"Query Quality, Performance & Validation","goal":"Write SQL that is correct, maintainable, and conscious of performance.","reason":"A fast incorrect report is worse than a slower audited one; reliability comes before indexing and speed work.","model":"Picture it this way: Validation is the safety check before optimization. A query that returns quickly but duplicates transactions is still incorrect.","steps":["Check total row count and distinct order IDs","Check the five qualifying completed records and their combined revenue","Only then inspect EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN or try an index"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Intentionally join orders to a duplicate reference table, then use row counts to diagnose inflated totals.","insight":"Read the expected result: The input has six order rows, six distinct order IDs and 578000 in completed revenue. Keep these numbers as independent controls after any rewrite.","trap":"EXPLAIN output is platform-specific, and an index is not inherently better for tiny tables."},"10":{"title":"Final SQL Analytics Project","goal":"Answer a realistic business brief using clean, validated SQL.","reason":"An analyst must supply business context, definitions, reproducible queries and evidence—not only a screenshot of a chart.","model":"Picture it this way: A production analytics deliverable is more than SQL syntax: it connects a business question, an agreed metric definition, verifiable output and a handoff note.","steps":["Record the question and inclusion rule","Run detail, regional and monthly views that reuse the same completed-sales definition","Reconcile regional revenue 578000 and profit 135000 to the source"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Deliver three SQL files plus a one-page README defining revenue, profit, returned-order treatment and control totals.","insight":"Read the expected result: The region summary accounts for all five completed orders. Document limitations: six invented orders are suitable for practice, not real market conclusions.","trap":"Do not treat this six-row illustrative sample as evidence about real customer behavior."}},"powerbi":{"1":{"title":"Power BI Foundations & Interface","goal":"Understand how Power BI works before building anything.","reason":"Power BI is valuable when stakeholders need governed measures, reusable relationships, and interactive slicing beyond a static spreadsheet.","model":"Picture it this way: Power BI has four layers: source files, Power Query preparation, the semantic model, and reports. Errors introduced in an earlier layer can appear as surprising visuals later.","steps":["Write the audience's business question","Define one row of FactSales and its grain","Draft a measure list before choosing visuals"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Sketch one executive report page with three KPIs, a month trend and regional breakdown; state the questions each answers.","insight":"Read the expected result: Six raw orders include one return. The completed revenue card should show 578000, which gives the model a simple acceptance test.","trap":"A visual can look correct while using implicit sums on the wrong transaction statuses."},"2":{"title":"Power Query for Data Cleaning","goal":"Prepare messy source data before it reaches the model.","reason":"Repeated CSV imports arrive with text-typed numbers, inconsistent columns and malformed dates. The refresh process should apply the same transformations reliably.","model":"Picture it this way: Power Query is a repeatable recipe, not a one-off cleanup. Each Applied Step transforms the table during refresh.","steps":["Import source CSV without altering it","Set Date, whole-number and numeric column types explicitly","Filter or flag statuses only where the business model requires it"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add a custom Revenue column in Power Query, then explain why it must not double count when DAX measures are used.","insight":"Read the expected result: The preview should contain six orders, including the returned one. Losing that record in preparation removes your ability to analyze returns later.","trap":"Automatic Changed Type may misinterpret day/month locale; explicitly validate dates and currencies."},"3":{"title":"Data Modeling & Star Schema","goal":"Build a model that is easy to calculate, filter, and maintain.","reason":"Business slicers for customers, regions and time should filter a consistent fact table without causing duplicated totals.","model":"Picture it this way: A star schema places transactions in the center and descriptive lookup tables around them. A relationship carries a filter from a dimension to a fact table.","steps":["Confirm FactSales has unique order IDs at the fact grain","Confirm DimCustomer has one row per customer_id","Create one-to-many relationships and test filter direction"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Count unique customer IDs in the customer dimension; explain why duplicate dimension keys are dangerous.","insight":"Read the expected result: Selecting Region=East and Status=Completed should leave only orders 1001 and 1006, producing revenue 316000.","trap":"Many-to-many keys and indiscriminate bidirectional filters can cause ambiguous paths or incorrect totals."},"4":{"title":"DAX Measures & Core Calculations","goal":"Create business metrics that respond to report filters.","reason":"A table visual and a KPI card must use the same revenue definition while respecting each report selection.","model":"Picture it this way: A DAX measure is a question answered again for the current filter context. Its value can change when a visual's region or date selection changes.","steps":["Define row revenue with SUMX over qualifying orders","Create a separate Profit measure using unit cost","Compare totals on a card versus inside a region visual"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Explain why SUM(FactSales[unit_price]) does not calculate revenue when quantity varies.","insight":"Read the expected result: No slicer: revenue 578000 and profit 135000. East slicer: revenue 316000. The formula stays the same; the filter context changes.","trap":"SUM(FactSales[quantity]*FactSales[unit_price]) is invalid DAX; use SUMX for an expression."},"5":{"title":"Date Tables & Time Intelligence","goal":"Compare performance across months, years, and prior periods.","reason":"Managers compare this month with previous months; string dates and missing calendar rows make time-intelligence results unreliable.","model":"Picture it this way: A date table is a complete calendar, not merely the dates where an order happened. Time intelligence depends on that consistent time axis.","steps":["Generate a date table with all dates in the reporting period","Mark it as the Date table and relate it to order_date","Create month-to-month measures only after validating the relationship"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Test the Date table by showing February dates with no orders and explaining why the calendar still includes them.","insight":"Read the expected result: January shows 340000 and February 238000. February's decrease is 30%; distinguish monthly revenue from a cumulative total.","trap":"Using FactSales[order_date] text or a noncontiguous date list defeats reliable period calculations."},"6":{"title":"Data Visualization & Report Design","goal":"Choose visuals that communicate clearly and build a readable report page.","reason":"Report readers need to spot variance and drill into drivers, not decode twelve disconnected charts.","model":"Picture it this way: Good visual design reduces the amount of mental arithmetic a manager must do. Labels, scales, reading order and color all carry meaning.","steps":["Place headline KPIs first","Use a monthly line chart and sorted regional bars","Add purposeful titles and tooltips that define each metric"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Replace a crowded multi-color chart with a restrained palette and descriptive data labels, then explain your design choice.","insight":"Read the expected result: The month trend goes from 340000 to 238000. Label it as a small training-data comparison, not proof of business deterioration.","trap":"A red card is not meaningful without a documented threshold or previous-period comparator."},"7":{"title":"Interactivity, Drillthrough & Bookmarks","goal":"Help users explore the report without editing it.","reason":"Operational managers often need to click a region, inspect customer-level drivers and return to the same overview.","model":"Picture it this way: A slicer changes filter context; drillthrough moves the reader from summary to details while carrying selected fields.","steps":["Create a Region slicer","Configure a drillthrough page filtered by Region","Test select, drill through and reset in a repeatable sequence"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Add a visible Reset Filters action and confirm that returning to all regions restores 578000.","insight":"Read the expected result: When West is selected, order-level details should include 1002 and 1005 and the headline should read 190000.","trap":"A bookmark that captures data filters unintentionally can reset slicers the user wanted to preserve."},"8":{"title":"Practical Intermediate DAX","goal":"Add ranking, averages, target comparisons, and dynamic report behavior.","reason":"Leaders ask which region contributes the most and what a customer’s share is within selected periods.","model":"Picture it this way: CALCULATE modifies the filters used by a measure. ALL and REMOVEFILTERS can deliberately remove a filter; you must document which denominator you intend.","steps":["Start with [Revenue] in current region context","Build a denominator over all selected regions or all regions, deliberately","Divide using DIVIDE to handle a zero denominator"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Show how Region Share changes under a West-only selection for an all-regions versus selected-regions denominator.","insight":"Read the expected result: East share of all completed regional revenue is 316000/578000=54.67%. ALLSELECTED and ALL are not interchangeable when slicers are present.","trap":"ALL on the entire fact table can remove time/status context and inflate denominators."},"9":{"title":"Publishing, Refresh & Power BI Service","goal":"Move a Desktop report into the online Power BI environment.","reason":"Publishing is not complete if users lack access, the data stays stale, or confidential rows leak between teams.","model":"Picture it this way: Publishing is a controlled release: permissions, refresh credentials, data sensitivity and business ownership matter as much as correct charts.","steps":["Choose the workspace and owners","Verify sources, schedule and refresh history","Test a view-only user and inspect row-level security where required"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Write a deployment checklist with role, refresh timestamp, owner, access test and rollback action.","insight":"Read the expected result: Refreshing should preserve the baseline KPI checks on the unchanged practice files. Permissions should not expose confidential records.","trap":"Assuming a hidden report page is a security boundary; it is not."},"10":{"title":"Final Power BI Report Project (optional Service dashboard)","goal":"Build a complete management report from raw source to publish-ready output.","reason":"A reusable semantic model and a concise insight narrative demonstrate job-ready analytical judgment.","model":"Picture it this way: The capstone tests analysis, modelling, usability and governance as one continuous chain. Success means a stakeholder can trace an insight to source records.","steps":["Create an explicit metric contract","Build and validate the star schema and measures","Present a dashboard, drillthrough and documented refresh/access plan"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Prepare a handover containing a model diagram, measure dictionary, QA evidence and three business observations.","insight":"Read the expected result: Acceptance baseline: five completed orders, 578000 revenue and 135000 profit, with returned order 1003 excluded from those totals.","trap":"A beautiful visual with misconfigured relationships is not a successful model."}},"python":{"1":{"title":"Python setup, files and analyst mindset","goal":"Load a dataset reproducibly and inspect its structure.","reason":"Analysts use Python when work must be repeatable, combine multiple files, or scale beyond manual clicking. The first objective is a clean, runnable environment.","model":"Picture it this way: A Python project is a repeatable recipe for data. The interpreter runs your code, pandas holds data in a DataFrame, and a file path tells Python where to find the CSV.","steps":["Create a project folder with data and scripts subfolders","Create an environment and install pandas only inside it","Run the sample from the project directory and inspect shape, columns and dtypes"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Run print(orders.head(2).to_string(index=False)) and explain what each displayed column means.","insight":"Read the expected result: A shape of (6, 12) means six rows and twelve columns—not 6 columns and 12 rows. If you see FileNotFoundError, print Path.cwd() and inspect the filename before changing code.","trap":"FileNotFoundError often means the working directory is wrong; inspect Path.cwd() and the path first."},"2":{"title":"Variables, types and control flow","goal":"Translate a calculation rule into correct Python logic.","reason":"Reporting calculations rely on precise numeric types and conditions; errors can turn a returned order into recognized sales.","model":"Picture it this way: A variable is a labeled container for a value; a condition is a checkpoint that decides whether a row contributes. Numbers and text behave differently when added.","steps":["Start with a single order and calculate quantity × unit_price","Add a condition to keep only Completed orders","Apply the same rule across a collection with a generator expression"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Modify the example to print both completed order count and revenue, and verify the original two-record list gives count 1.","insight":"Read the expected result: For order 1001, 2 × 150000 = 300000. Returned order 1003 is ignored. Numeric strings such as '150000' must be converted before multiplication.","trap":"Using = inside a condition is a syntax error; use == for equality. Avoid comparing text values to numbers."},"3":{"title":"Functions, collections and reusable scripts","goal":"Write one calculation once and reuse it safely.","reason":"Analysts often repeat net-sales and margin logic across reports; functions prevent inconsistent copies.","model":"Picture it this way: A function turns one business rule into a reusable tool: inputs go in, calculations happen once, and an output comes back. A return value is different from printing.","steps":["Give the function meaningful parameters and a docstring","Check that quantity and price are valid numbers","Call the function on one order and test a second order independently"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Write a profit(quantity, unit_price, unit_cost) function and check order 1001 produces 60000.","insight":"Read the expected result: revenue(2, 150000) returns 300000 for order 1001. A unit test should catch incorrect types, negative quantities and unexpected rounding conventions.","trap":"Mutable default arguments such as def f(items=[]) can leak state between calls."},"4":{"title":"pandas selection and filtering","goal":"Filter the correct transactions before aggregation.","reason":"A pandas DataFrame brings SQL-like filtering to CSV data, allowing consistent calculations in automated pipelines.","model":"Picture it this way: A DataFrame is a spreadsheet-like table, but pandas operations explicitly create filtered views and grouped summaries. Boolean masks are row-selection rules.","steps":["Read the CSV and inspect the Status values","Create a completed-only DataFrame using a Boolean expression","Create a revenue column on a copy, then sum it"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Filter East completed orders, display order_id and revenue, and check the total is 316000.","insight":"Read the expected result: Five rows satisfy status=='Completed', yielding 578000. Use .copy() before adding columns to a sliced DataFrame to avoid ambiguous chained assignments.","trap":"Do not use `and` between pandas Series masks; use `&` with parentheses: (a) & (b)."},"5":{"title":"Cleaning, nulls, typing and joins","goal":"Protect an analytics pipeline against faulty imports.","reason":"Noisy files often carry blank customer IDs, mixed formats and duplicate keys. Failures should be reported, not silently discarded.","model":"Picture it this way: Data cleaning is a sequence of quality checks, not a command to delete missing rows. Missing values, wrong types and duplicate keys each need different treatment.","steps":["Check dtypes, missing counts and duplicate order IDs","Convert date and numeric fields using explicit rules","Validate join cardinality before merging customers with orders"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create a validation function that raises ValueError if duplicate order IDs are present; try it on a deliberately duplicated row.","insight":"Read the expected result: The training data has zero duplicate order IDs. An unchecked many-to-many merge can still multiply order rows and inflate reported revenue.","trap":"A merge on duplicate lookup keys can multiply transaction rows and inflate sales."},"6":{"title":"groupby, pivot and business KPI reporting","goal":"Generate a clear, reproducible regional summary.","reason":"An automated weekly report needs the same measure definitions as the SQL and Power BI versions.","model":"Picture it this way: groupby splits data into categories, computes a summary for each category, and combines the results. It is Python's equivalent of an SQL GROUP BY or an Excel PivotTable.","steps":["Filter to Completed first","Calculate row revenue and profit correctly","Group by region and aggregate to a labeled output table"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Return both revenue and profit by region, sort from highest revenue to lowest, and verify the profit total is 135000.","insight":"Read the expected result: Expected revenue: East 316000, West 190000, North 72000. The sum of groups must reconcile to overall completed revenue 578000.","trap":"groupby drops NULL grouping keys by default in pandas unless dropna=False is specified."},"7":{"title":"Statistics and uncertainty","goal":"Interpret metrics without making unsupported causal claims.","reason":"Executives often ask whether an apparent change reflects a durable pattern or random variation; statistics clarify what data can and cannot establish.","model":"Picture it this way: Statistics summarizes variation; it does not automatically prove causation. Mean uses all values, median is the middle ordered value, and uncertainty limits what you can claim.","steps":["Inspect distribution and sample size","Compute both mean and median instead of relying on one average","Say explicitly whether the data is a sample or a complete population"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Find the median completed-order revenue and explain which value better represents a typical small order.","insight":"Read the expected result: Five completed orders average 115600 each in revenue. One laptop order worth 300000 makes the distribution uneven; the mean alone can hide that skew.","trap":"Using the mean as a typical order is misleading when there is a large 300000 order; compare median."},"8":{"title":"NumPy vectors and matrix intuition","goal":"Recognize vectorized calculations and matrix dimensions.","reason":"Forecasting and machine learning depend on efficient numeric arrays; matrix dimensions explain common shape errors.","model":"Picture it this way: NumPy performs the same operation over many numbers at once. A vector is a one-dimensional array; a matrix is a two-dimensional grid with row and column counts.","steps":["Build matching quantity and price arrays","Multiply elementwise to obtain order revenues","Check array shapes before matrix multiplication or broadcasting"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create a parallel cost array and compute unit profits and total profit for the same three orders.","insight":"Read the expected result: Quantities [2,5,4] times prices [150000,8000,18000] yield [300000,40000,72000]. Shape mismatches are dimension errors, not rounding errors.","trap":"Broadcasting may silently stretch arrays; use shape checks instead of relying on assumptions."},"9":{"title":"Time series, charts and testable forecasts","goal":"Create a baseline with transparent limitations.","reason":"Forecasting decisions require a benchmark and careful time-aware validation, rather than presenting a clever model as automatically accurate.","model":"Picture it this way: Time-series analysis respects order and time. A forecasting method must be tested on later periods; evaluating on the same points used for fitting makes error look too small.","steps":["Convert order_date to a date type","Group Completed revenue into monthly periods","Build a naive baseline before discussing more complex forecasting models"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create a monthly bar chart with values labeled and add a note explaining the data limitation.","insight":"Read the expected result: January totals 340000 and February 238000. Two months of invented data are insufficient to establish seasonality or forecast quality.","trap":"Two months cannot support a reliable seasonal forecast; never advertise predictive accuracy from this demo."},"10":{"title":"Python analytics capstone","goal":"Package a repeatable, independently verified report.","reason":"Professional scripts deliver a consistent output and clear failure messages when inputs change.","model":"Picture it this way: A script is reproducible when someone can run it on the supplied CSV and obtain the documented result in a clean environment.","steps":["Write functions for load, validate, transform and report","Add assertions for schema, duplicate keys and totals","Export the regional KPI table and document how to rerun it"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Deliver a .py file, requirements.txt, README and report CSV, with one deliberately failing test case.","insight":"Read the expected result: East revenue is 316000 and profit 66000; all completed regions must total 578000 and 135000. Saved notebook cells are not enough—rerun from a fresh process.","trap":"Notebook outputs saved after a different execution order are not proof of reproducibility; run from a clean kernel."}},"ai":{"1":{"title":"AI literacy for analysts","goal":"Learning outcome: Understand generative AI, predictive ML and automation; differentiate a plausible answer from a reproducible result.","reason":"Analysts can use AI to outline hypotheses and draft code quickly, but confident prose is not evidence that a number is correct.","model":"Picture it this way: An AI assistant predicts useful language, not the truth of a calculation. Think of it as a drafting partner whose output needs a separate evidence trail.","steps":["State the analytics task and available data","Ask the assistant to describe a reproducible method","Run that method in Excel, SQL or Python before trusting the number"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Write one prompt asking for an analysis plan and another asking for a calculated answer; label which claims need execution.","insight":"Read the expected result: 578000 completed revenue is verified by the supplied data and scripts; it is not verified merely because an AI response mentions it.","trap":"An LLM that claims it \"checked the data\" without actually using a tool has not verified it."},"2":{"title":"Prompt engineering and analytical questions","goal":"Learning outcome: Write prompts with business context, data schema, task, constraints, outputs and tests.","reason":"A vague prompt such as \"analyse my sales\" produces generic prose, whereas a specification defines the business question and acceptance tests.","model":"Picture it this way: A good prompt functions like a mini requirements document. Without table names, business definitions and output constraints, the assistant must guess.","steps":["Give the exact schema and grain","Define which statuses count as sales","Request the output format plus independent tests and edge cases"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Rewrite a vague 'show me sales' prompt into a testable specification for regional completed revenue.","insight":"Read the expected result: A correct SQL draft filters Completed before grouping by region. AI-generated code that invents nonexistent columns must be rejected rather than silently adapted.","trap":"Without specifying SQL dialect, AI may suggest unsupported DATE_TRUNC or TOP syntax for SQLite."},"3":{"title":"Data security, privacy and responsible AI","goal":"Learning outcome: Apply confidentiality tiers, anonymisation, tool policies and human sign-off.","reason":"Customers, employee records and proprietary transactions may be confidential; disclosure must follow organizational policy and law.","model":"Picture it this way: Data minimization means sharing only what is necessary. Synthetic practice records are safe for exercises; real people, credentials or protected records require authorization and controls.","steps":["Classify each field by sensitivity","Replace personal identifiers with approved aggregates when possible","Review vendor retention, sharing permissions and organization policy"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Produce a safe three-column aggregate (region, completed_orders, revenue) without customer names.","insight":"Read the expected result: The fictional dataset can be used for demonstrations; a real customer list or patient record cannot automatically be uploaded to external AI tools.","trap":"Replacing names with IDs alone may not anonymize rare or linkable personal records."},"4":{"title":"AI-assisted Excel and Power Query","goal":"Learning outcome: Accelerate spreadsheet formulas and transformations while checking every output.","reason":"AI can explain formula options, but a workbook still must use supported functions and the correct source table.","model":"Picture it this way: AI can suggest Excel formulas, but the spreadsheet engine and business rules still determine the answer. A formula can be syntactically correct while counting returns.","steps":["Specify Excel version and actual Table column names","Request the formula with an explanation for each part","Test returned-order and completed-order scenarios"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Ask AI to propose a SUMIFS formula, then audit it against the order IDs 1001 and 1006.","insight":"Read the expected result: East completed revenue must equal 316000. XLOOKUP is not available in all Excel 2016 builds; ask for compatible alternatives where needed.","trap":"XLOOKUP is not available in Excel 2016; suggest INDEX/MATCH or VLOOKUP exact-match."},"5":{"title":"AI-assisted SQL and database analysis","goal":"Learning outcome: Produce, explain, debug and optimise SQL without permitting invented schema.","reason":"Text generators may guess joins, columns or dialect-specific functions. Giving a schema changes the exercise from creative writing to constrained code review.","model":"Picture it this way: Schema-grounded prompting prevents the assistant from making up a customers table or fictional fields. SQL must match the actual database dialect.","steps":["Supply table DDL and a sample of representative values","Ask for a read-only SQLite query","Execute it, inspect intermediate rows and reconcile totals"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Request a query that detects duplicate order IDs and compare its expected result to the included SQLite file.","insight":"Read the expected result: The validated monthly result is January 340000 and February 238000. Any query returning a third month has used a different filter or input.","trap":"A tool-generated query can run successfully but still count returned orders; execution success is not business correctness."},"6":{"title":"AI-assisted Python analytics","goal":"Learning outcome: Use AI to scaffold auditable pandas notebooks and plots.","reason":"Python automation is valuable but copying code blindly creates silent errors and brittle scripts.","model":"Picture it this way: AI is often most helpful at explaining a traceable error message. It should not remove validation checks or change a metric definition just to make code run.","steps":["Provide a minimal failing code snippet and traceback","Ask for the root cause and smallest safe fix","Run the fixed version and compare against a known expected result"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Create an intentionally broken pandas filter and request a diagnosis that explains why parentheses are needed.","insight":"Read the expected result: East has two completed order rows. A common pandas error is using Python 'and' rather than '&' between Boolean Series conditions.","trap":"Python `and` cannot combine pandas Series masks; use `&` with parentheses."},"7":{"title":"AI-assisted Power BI, DAX and storytelling","goal":"Learning outcome: Design semantic models, DAX measures and useful stakeholder narratives.","reason":"AI can suggest DAX formulas that look correct but change when slicers filter the model.","model":"Picture it this way: DAX questions depend on relationships and current filter context, which are often invisible in a pasted formula. Prompting without model metadata can produce plausible wrong answers.","steps":["Describe model tables and relationship directions","Supply the exact measure and slicer situation","Require a card-versus-region validation case"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Ask AI to review one CALCULATE denominator choice and design a slicer test that would expose an error.","insight":"Read the expected result: [Revenue] should show 578000 overall and 316000 for East. A formula using SUM(unit_price) ignores quantity and fails even if it parses.","trap":"Removing all fact filters to fix a single result may erase legitimate date/region selections."},"8":{"title":"Statistical reasoning, ML and forecasting","goal":"Learning outcome: Understand when to use regression, clustering, forecasts and anomaly detection.","reason":"Forecasts are high-impact because decision makers may act on them; AI-generated accuracy claims require a real test design.","model":"Picture it this way: Predictive claims need out-of-sample evidence. An attractive chart or confident paragraph cannot compensate for data leakage or a tiny sample.","steps":["Establish a naive baseline","Separate training from later test observations","Review suitable metrics and intervals without claiming causality"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Ask the assistant to critique a forecast trained and tested on the same two monthly observations.","insight":"Read the expected result: The six-order training sample is for explaining workflow, not a credible production forecasting experiment. MAE is the average absolute distance between predicted and actual values.","trap":"Mixing future target data into training features creates deceptively optimistic accuracy."},"9":{"title":"Automation, agents and workflow efficiency","goal":"Learning outcome: Design repeatable AI-supported workflows with explicit approval gates.","reason":"Scheduled reporting can save analyst hours, but an automated agent must not silently overwrite records or send incorrect figures.","model":"Picture it this way: An automated agent can draft, transform and route work, but approvals must remain explicit where outputs affect money, records or external systems.","steps":["Validate source schema before every run","Log data version, prompts, outputs and control totals","Route anomalies to a human before publication or a write action"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Design an approval gate that rejects missing columns, duplicate IDs or unverified totals.","insight":"Read the expected result: If completed revenue changes unexpectedly from 578000 on the unchanged sample, the workflow should stop and request investigation rather than publish.","trap":"Automatic publication without permission, source validation or rollback creates avoidable operational risk."},"10":{"title":"Capstone: governed AI-augmented analytics","goal":"Learning outcome: Deliver an evidence-based analysis showing useful, safe and measurable AI assistance.","reason":"A credible AI portfolio submission shows how tools accelerated work without replacing analytical responsibility.","model":"Picture it this way: A responsible AI analytics project makes a clear distinction between assistance, computation and human judgment. Its audit trail matters as much as the final slide.","steps":["Record prompts and AI-generated drafts","Execute trusted calculations and reconcile figures","Document who approved the narrative and any deployment decision"],"practice":"Now try it without copying: Prepare an evidence pack with one prompt, corrected script, test result, privacy note and executive summary.","insight":"Read the expected result: Accepted baseline is five completed orders, revenue 578000 and profit 135000. AI-generated business claims must be checked against those executed facts.","trap":"Concealing invented figures, sensitive inputs or unreviewed code invalidates the demonstration."}},"introduction":{"1":{"title":"What is data analysis?","goal":"Explain the difference between raw data, a calculated summary, an insight and an action.","reason":"A shop can record hundreds of sales without knowing what to stock tomorrow. Analysis turns those records into an evidence-based decision.","model":"Three transport costs of ₦2,000, ₦3,000 and ₦1,000 are data; ₦6,000 is a summary; Tuesday was most expensive is an insight; trying a cheaper Tuesday route is an action.","steps":["Identify a record and the question it could answer","Calculate or compare the values accurately","Write one insight and the decision it could inform"],"practice":"Try it (2 minutes) Write down one number you record in daily life, a question it could answer, and one decision you might make after checking the answer.","insight":"One-minute example You spent ₦2,000, ₦3,000 and ₦1,000 on transport over three days. The records are data . Adding them gives ₦6,000 , a summary. Noticing that Tuesday was the most expensive day is an insight . Checking whether a cheaper route works on Tuesdays is a pos","trap":""},"2":{"title":"Why learn data analysis? You already use it.","goal":"Recognise opportunities to use data analysis in routine personal and business decisions.","reason":"Data literacy helps you compare costs, measure improvement, and ask what changed rather than relying on assumptions.","model":"When a food seller changes packet size, compare price per gram or unit rather than the total packet price; a cheaper-looking packet can cost more per serving.","steps":["Choose a repeated activity worth measuring","Record the same fields consistently across occasions","Compare a fair metric and explain the limitation"],"practice":"Try it (5 minutes) Think of a purchase you make regularly. Record its price and quantity on three occasions. Ask whether the unit price , rather than only the package price, has increased.","insight":"Why employers value these skills Teams in finance, operations, logistics, education, marketing and health all need people who can define a metric, find reliable evidence, check calculations and explain results clearly. The ability to ask good questions and communicate l","trap":""},"3":{"title":"How to turn a question into a decision","goal":"Turn a vague concern into a specific, measurable question with an appropriate decision.","reason":"A well-defined question determines which records, dates and calculations are relevant and prevents a misleading analysis.","model":"“Transport is expensive” is vague. “Which of two routes has the lower average cost over the past four weeks?” gives a population, metric, comparison and period.","steps":["Identify who or what the question concerns","Specify the metric, comparison and time period","Decide how the evidence would influence an action"],"practice":"Try it (3 minutes) Rewrite “My transport is expensive” as a measurable question. Include a cost, a comparison and a period—for example, the average weekly cost for two possible routes over four weeks.","insight":"Better questions produce better answers Weak: “Do customers like my shop?” Better: “Of the 30 customers who answered our anonymous survey this week, how many rated waiting time as satisfactory?” That answer only represents those who responded—not necessarily every custo","trap":""},"4":{"title":"Understand rows, columns and what numbers mean","goal":"Read simple tables and distinguish records, attributes, counts and totals.","reason":"Counting rows is not the same as counting distinct customers or products. Treating them as equal can distort a report.","model":"Three purchase records can involve only two different items. A row describes a purchase, while columns describe its date, item and amount.","steps":["State what one row represents","Identify the meaning and units of each column","Calculate both row count and distinct-item count before a total"],"practice":"Try it (4 minutes) Using the purchase table: count rows, count distinct items, then sum the three amounts. Answer: 3 purchases, 2 items and ₦9,700 total spending .","insight":"","trap":""},"5":{"title":"Clean, check and protect data","goal":"Inspect incomplete or duplicate records, validate numbers and respect data privacy.","reason":"Bad identifiers and missing values can create false conclusions; removing records without investigation may destroy legitimate evidence.","model":"Two receipts with the same ID but different dates could be two sales with a typo. Check original receipts before deciding whether either record is a duplicate.","steps":["Check data types, missing fields and unrealistic values","Investigate duplicates against source evidence","Document the correction and avoid exposing private information"],"practice":"Try it (5 minutes) A spending sheet has two identical receipt IDs but different dates. Would you delete one automatically? No. First check the original receipts. They might be a true duplicate or two legitimate purchases with a bad ID.","insight":"","trap":""},"6":{"title":"Six simple calculations that answer real questions","goal":"Use sums, averages, differences and percentages to describe business performance correctly.","reason":"Comparisons depend on the denominator. A change of ₦3,000 means more when compared with a ₦15,000 budget than a ₦150,000 budget.","model":"Spending ₦18,000 instead of ₦15,000 gives a ₦3,000 overrun. Dividing ₦3,000 by the original budget gives a 20% increase.","steps":["Choose the calculation that answers the decision question","Show the units and the original comparison value","Check the result using a second method or estimate"],"practice":"Try it (5 minutes) You budgeted ₦15,000 for data but spent ₦18,000. Calculate the difference ( ₦3,000 ) and percentage increase relative to the budget ( 20% ). Explain whether your answer describes spending or profit.","insight":"","trap":""},"7":{"title":"A small lunch kiosk: from receipts to a decision","goal":"Analyse a small retail kiosk using revenue, costs, profit and break-even quantities.","reason":"High sales do not guarantee profit. A kiosk owner must understand how selling price, variable costs and fixed costs combine.","model":"Selling for ₦1,500 with ₦900 variable cost leaves ₦600 contribution per meal. With ₦6,000 fixed costs, 10 meals cover costs but do not yet generate profit.","steps":["Calculate revenue from meals sold × selling price","Subtract variable and fixed costs to find profit","Estimate break-even and test how changing sales volume affects the answer"],"practice":"Your independent practice (15 minutes) Without checking the totals, calculate Wednesday’s profit from the downloaded CSV. Calculate the week’s total revenue and profit margin. Change Monday’s meals sold to 8 in the calculator: is the result a profit or a loss? Write one","insight":"Contribution per meal ₦1,500 selling price − ₦900 variable cost = ₦600 per meal toward fixed costs and profit. Daily break-even quantity = ₦6,000 ÷ ₦600 = 10 meals . At exactly 10 meals, profit is ₦0 under these simplified assumptions.","trap":""},"8":{"title":"Where Excel, SQL and Power BI fit","goal":"Explain how Excel, SQL and Power BI support different stages of analysis.","reason":"Tools matter less than the analytical sequence: define the question, obtain trusted evidence, calculate, communicate and recommend.","model":"Excel is useful for hands-on inspection and calculations, SQL retrieves and combines larger tables, and Power BI builds reports that stakeholders can explore.","steps":["Describe the problem without naming a tool","Match each analytical stage to a practical tool","Prepare a short story connecting a finding to a responsible decision"],"practice":"Try it (5 minutes) Tell a friend what an analyst did in the kiosk example without mentioning Excel, SQL or Power BI. Describe the question, evidence, calculation, finding and responsible next step.","insight":"When are you ready for the main courses? When you can explain what a row represents, check a total, distinguish revenue from profit, and state one limitation, you are ready to build on these skills. The first recommended technical course is Excel; SQL and Power BI follo","trap":""}}};
